=== worker/AGENTS.md ===
# Worker — AI Agent Notes

The second process of this project (ADR-0018 of Puerto). The API's units of work write every
event to the `outbox` table in the entity's own transaction; this process **relays** the outbox
to the transport and **runs the consumers**. It is a composition root, like `presentation`: no
business logic lives here — a consumer decodes the event and calls a use case.

## Layer Structure

```
worker/src/
├── main.rs                  # `WorkerConfig::from_env()` + `Worker::new(config).try_run(…)` (never changes)
├── generated.rs             # `pub mod registry;`
├── generated/registry.rs    # AUTO-GENERATED: the relay and every `[[consumer]]` — never hand-edit
├── consumers.rs             # `pub mod <consumer>;` — no `mod.rs`
└── consumers/
    └── <consumer>.rs        # Yours: an `EventHandler`, written once by `puerto generate consumer`
```

## Critical Rules

- **Add a consumer with `puerto generate consumer <name> --on <event_type>[,…]`** — it declares
  `[[consumer]]` in `puerto.toml`, writes the handler and its tests, and regenerates
  `generated/registry.rs`. Never wire one by hand.
- **Handlers must be idempotent.** Delivery is at-least-once: the same `event.id` can arrive
  twice. Check whether the work was already done (keyed on `event.id`) before doing it again.
- **Answer honestly**: `Ack` when done; `Retry` for a failure that may pass (redelivered with a
  1 s → 300 s backoff, `MAX_DELIVERIES` times); `HandlerOutcome::dead_letter(reason, detail)` for
  one that never will. A payload that does not decode is `REASON_PAYLOAD_INVALID`. Nothing is
  dropped without a row in `dead_letters` (`make events/dead-letters`).
- **Decode the snapshot, never the domain model**: `event.decode::<ProductSnapshot>()`. The
  snapshot is the event's public contract.

## Health and shutdown

Served by the Puerto runtime, `puerto-events`, on `WORKER_PORT` (8081).

- `GET /health` — liveness: the process answers.
- `GET /health/ready` — readiness: `503` until the relay and every consumer have finished a pass
  that did not fail recently (a database that is down shows up here).
- On SIGTERM / Ctrl-C each loop finishes the batch it is handling, for up to 8 s.

## Configuration

`WORKER_PORT` (8081), `RELAY_BATCH_SIZE` (100), `RELAY_POLL_INTERVAL_MS` (500),
`CONSUMER_POLL_INTERVAL_MS` (500), `MAX_DELIVERIES` (5), `RETRY_MAX_DELAY_MS` (unset: the backoff
above), plus `DATABASE_URL`. A value that is set but invalid stops the worker at startup.

=== worker/Cargo.toml ===
[package]
name = "worker"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"

[dependencies]
business = { path = "../business" }
infrastructure = { path = "../infrastructure" }
puerto-core = { workspace = true }
# The relay, the consume loops, health probes and graceful shutdown (`main.rs`, `generated/registry.rs`).
puerto-events = { workspace = true }
async-trait = "0.1"
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["rt-multi-thread", "macros"] }
tracing = "0.1"

[dev-dependencies]
# Consumer tests build envelopes from the Object Mothers, which business exports behind `test-utils`.
business = { path = "../business", features = ["test-utils"] }
serde_json = "1"

=== worker/src/consumers.rs ===
//! One module per `[[consumer]]` of puerto.toml — `puerto generate consumer` adds them.

=== worker/src/generated.rs ===
pub mod registry;

=== worker/src/generated/registry.rs ===
// AUTO-GENERATED by puerto. Edit puerto.toml, then run `puerto generate bootstrap`; add a
// consumer with `puerto generate consumer <name> --on <event_type>`.
use infrastructure::db::events::{PgDeliveries, PgOutbox};
use puerto_events::{Loops, WorkerConfig};

/// The relay and one consumer per `[[consumer]]`, on the `db` transport: the relay hands each
/// event to `event_deliveries`, one row per consumer subscribed to its type.
pub async fn build_worker(config: WorkerConfig) -> Result<Loops, String> {
    let database_url = std::env::var("DATABASE_URL").map_err(|_| {
        "DATABASE_URL is not set — set it in .env (.env.example has one)".to_string()
    })?;
    let pool = infrastructure::db::create_postgres_pool(&database_url)
        .await
        .map_err(|e| format!("{e} — is the database up? `make docker-compose/up`"))?;
    infrastructure::db::run_migrations(&pool, &infrastructure::db::MIGRATOR)
        .await
        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;

    Ok(Loops::new(&config).relay(PgOutbox::new(pool.clone()), PgDeliveries::new(pool)))
}

=== worker/src/main.rs ===
mod consumers;
mod generated;

/// The second process of this project (ADR-0018 of Puerto): it relays the outbox the API writes
/// and runs the consumers. The loops, health probes and graceful shutdown come from the Puerto
/// runtime, `puerto-events`; the wiring is in `generated/registry.rs`, the consumers in
/// `consumers/`.
#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), puerto_events::Error> {
    let config = puerto_events::WorkerConfig::from_env()?;
    puerto_events::Worker::new(config)
        .try_run(generated::registry::build_worker)
        .await
}
